# r16 FRICTION (known, -i stage 1: the evaluator never frees) - B3e: ordinary scripting data sizes exhaust memory under
# -i. This loop builds and drops one short line 20,000 times; built, it runs in a few MB. Measured on the study's
# programs: a log analyser over 300 lines peaks at 1.47 GB under -i and is killed at 5,000 lines; an LRU simulation of
# 1,000 steps peaks at 2.0 GB, and 20,000 steps is killed; a 20,000-line word count is killed before it reaches its
# tasks. Principle 5 lists -i first among the scripting tools.
#
# expected: "done 200000" under -i, in bounded memory
# actual: -b prints it at once; -i peaks at 1.46 GB for 2,000 iterations (7 s) and is killed at 12.3 GB for 20,000
#         (peak resident memory polled from /proc)
fn main() ? {
    n I64 = 0
    for i in range 20000 {
        line := "entry " $i " of the log, with some words in it"
        n += line.Split(" ").Len()
    }
    println("done " $n)
}
